{
  "method_card": {
    "steps": [
      "Identify the data type (categorical, discrete, continuous, bivariate)",
      "Choose the right chart type",
      "For reading charts: check axes, scale, and labels carefully",
      "For pie charts: use the angle or fraction to find the frequency"
    ],
    "title": "Representing Data",
    "content": "<p><strong>Bar charts:</strong> Bars represent frequencies. Heights are proportional. Use for categorical or discrete data.</p><p><strong>Pie charts:</strong> Angle per category = \\(\\frac{\\text{frequency}}{\\text{total}} \\times 360°\\). To read: angle ÷ 360 × total.</p><p><strong>Scatter graphs:</strong> Plot bivariate data. <strong>Correlation:</strong> positive (both increase), negative (one increases, other decreases), or none. The <strong>line of best fit</strong> should pass through \\((\\bar{x}, \\bar{y})\\). <strong>Interpolation</strong> (within range) is reliable; <strong>extrapolation</strong> (outside range) is unreliable.</p><p><strong>Frequency polygons</strong> and <strong>time series</strong> show trends over grouped data or time.</p>",
    "example": "<p><strong>A pie chart represents 120 people. The 'Bus' sector is 90°. How many travel by bus?</strong></p><p>\\(\\frac{90}{360} \\times 120 = 30\\) people</p>"
  },
  "topic_links": {
    "prerequisites": []
  },
